    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-binary-composition",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.binary-composition",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-binary-composition",
      "layer": "composition",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.composition",
        "eg.derived-data-lifecycle",
        "eg.feature-graph",
        "eg.architecture-gates",
        "eg.release-composition"
      ],
      "summary": "Feature selection, process lifecycle and the gate suite. Owns the root Cargo.toml feature graph (default = graph, algorithms, metrics, full; full is an 84-entry list that INCLUDES security and the viz family), the five [[bin]] targets each behind required-features (epistemic-graph-server/server, nemesis/harness, migrate-shards, restore/redb+server, lake-fixture-export/lake), .cargo/config.toml (target-dir = target-isolated, the GGML CPU portability contract, mold, sccache), arch-lint.toml, the 60-hook .config/pre-commit.yaml and scripts/. It is therefore the owner of every architecture-gate decision: D-EG-004 (no crate-DAG gate exists), D-EG-006 (the arch-lint exclude misses target-isolated) and the sequencing half of D-EG-009."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-cluster-replication",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.cluster-replication",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-cluster-replication",
      "layer": "storage-transaction",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.raft-consensus",
        "eg.shard-placement",
        "eg.cross-shard-transaction",
        "eg.cluster-elasticity"
      ],
      "summary": "The clustering, consensus and replication subsystem (23 modules): node/network/store, membership shrink, drain, reshard, placement, cross-node elasticity, cross-shard transactions, exchange, xread, Pregel, and their harnesses. Like eg.persistence this seam is entirely absent from the epistemic-graph-development skill's layer model, and it is the third-largest AL002 owner. Recorded here so the burndown has an owner."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-composition",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.composition",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-composition",
      "layer": "composition",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.composition",
        "eg.derived-data-lifecycle"
      ],
      "childComponent": [
        "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-observability",
        "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-binary-composition"
      ],
      "summary": "RF-022 layer boundary root seam, carried forward unchanged from the non-authoritative fixture at plans/refactor/architecture/components/epistemic-graph.yml. It owns no source path; the implementation components listed in child_component_ids do."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-compute",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.compute",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-compute",
      "layer": "query-reasoning",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.compute-domains",
        "eg.graph-algorithms",
        "eg.datalog-reasoning"
      ],
      "summary": "Compute domains: graph algorithms (always compiled), AST/tree-sitter, finance, datascience and Datalog reasoning. Consumed by eg-query, eg-epistemic, eg-plan, eg-tsdb and (optionally) eg-kvcache -- the last two are the D-EG-002 edges."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-core",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.core",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-core",
      "layer": "core",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.graph-invariants",
        "eg.identity-rbac-admission",
        "eg.graph-acl-isolation",
        "eg.graph-policy-lease"
      ],
      "childComponent": [
        "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-graph-core"
      ],
      "summary": "RF-022 layer boundary root seam, carried forward unchanged from the non-authoritative fixture at plans/refactor/architecture/components/epistemic-graph.yml. It owns no source path; the implementation components listed in child_component_ids do."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-embedded-bindings",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.embedded-bindings",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-embedded-bindings",
      "layer": "server-protocol",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.embedded-store",
        "eg.pyo3-inprocess-binding",
        "eg.language-clients"
      ],
      "summary": "In-process and out-of-process language bindings: the embedded store surface, the PyO3 in-process binding (opt-in only -- the out-of-process build must never link it, enforced by scripts/check_no_pyo3.sh), the Python client package (governed by .importlinter) and the JS client (governed by clients/js/.dependency-cruiser.cjs, which is the ONLY thing the dependency-cruiser-architecture hook validates -- see D-EG-004)."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-epistemic",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.epistemic",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-epistemic",
      "layer": "query-reasoning",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.claims-evidence-belief",
        "eg.contradiction-tms"
      ],
      "summary": "Claims, evidence, belief-state, contradiction detection, confidence propagation and justification (a truth-maintenance system), plus the facade's reasoning cascade and reasoning projection that serve it."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-graph-core",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.graph-core",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-graph-core",
      "layer": "core",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.graph-invariants",
        "eg.identity-rbac-admission",
        "eg.graph-acl-isolation",
        "eg.graph-policy-lease",
        "eg.rls-view-cache",
        "eg.semantic-indexing"
      ],
      "summary": "GraphCore (petgraph graph + ledger), GraphRegistry (multi-tenant), isolation.rs (the zero-trust ACL, AccessLevel/RowVisibility/check_access/can_see_row/filter_view and mint_policy_decision_lease), SemanticStore, rls_view_cache.rs and rbac_persist.rs. Per RF-022/RF-010's four-boundary ruling this is the SOLE deciding authority for durable RBAC/identity admission, graph ACL / tenant isolation / row visibility, and policy-decision-lease minting; eg.query-plan, eg.persistence and eg.server-dispatch are consumers that call lease.filter_view()/validate_before/validate_after, never a second deciding authority."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-job-plane",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.job-plane",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-job-plane",
      "layer": "storage-transaction",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.analytics-job-plane",
        "eg.statechart-engine",
        "eg.durable-work-item"
      ],
      "summary": "The durable analytics-job plane over a redb-backed AnalyticsJob state machine (committing results as provenance'd claims through GraphCore), plus its disciplined sibling, the formal FSM/statechart engine and its durable machine-instance store. The three facade-level statechart definitions (work item, loop, agent lifecycle) are owned here because they are chart DEFINITIONS over this engine, not server dispatch."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-kv-cache",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.kv-cache",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-kv-cache",
      "layer": "storage-transaction",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.tiered-kv-cache"
      ],
      "summary": "Tiered hot/warm/cold KV-cache store plus the content-addressed shared multi-instance backend. Its edge to eg-compute is OPTIONAL behind the off-by-default `graph` feature (graph-guided paging) and is NOT in the default or `full` feature closure -- see D-EG-002."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-lake-interop",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.lake-interop",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-lake-interop",
      "layer": "storage-transaction",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.lakehouse-interop",
        "eg.sqlite-file-format"
      ],
      "summary": "Foreign storage-format interop: the LTAP lakehouse leg (Parquet + Delta + Iceberg + Iceberg-REST catalog) and the pure-Rust SQLite .db file-format reader/bulk-load writer (no C, no libsqlite3). Both are format adapters with no in-engine store authority; grouped because both exist solely to read and write somebody else's on-disk format."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-lm-program",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.lm-program",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-lm-program",
      "layer": "types",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.lm-program-contract"
      ],
      "summary": "Graph-native governed LM-program and self-improvement contracts (deps = eg-modality, eg-types). Contract-only; wired into the facade behind the root `program-optimization` feature. Model- and vendor-agnostic by construction, per the layout contract's prohibition on vendor labels in ownership identity."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-modality",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.modality",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-modality",
      "layer": "types",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.modality-contract",
        "eg.governed-media-decode",
        "eg.typed-document-structure",
        "eg.geospatial-predicates",
        "eg.lexical-index",
        "eg.stream-cep",
        "eg.evidence-alignment",
        "eg.tensor-modality"
      ],
      "summary": "The ModalityContract seam and every crate that converges on it. eg-modality owns the trait + conformance harness (TCK) and deliberately sits BELOW eg-plan/eg-core with deps = eg-types only. The leaves own governed pure-Rust decode + evidence: PCM/WAV, PNG + pHash, ISOBMFF + RGB frames, typed document pages/layout/tables, WKT geometry + an in-house packed Hilbert R-tree (no GEOS/PROJ), a Tantivy BM25 inverted index, a bounded-NFA CEP engine, and the shared alignment graph over EvidenceLocus. eg-tensor is owned here as the dense N-D array modality but carries a declared layer violation (see D-EG-002): its edge to eg-tsdb is mandatory while its edge to eg-modality is OPTIONAL behind the `contract` feature."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-mutation-store",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.mutation-store",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-mutation-store",
      "layer": "types",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.mutation-ledger",
        "eg.mutation-scope-binding",
        "eg.private-payload-seal",
        "eg.store-incarnation-identity",
        "eg.store-relocation-adoption"
      ],
      "summary": "RF-RULING-004's two dependency-ordered authorities, formerly one crate (eg-mutation-store, retired 2026-09-06 -- see migration_lineage). eg-transaction (\"Sole mutation authority: admission, ordering, fencing, commit, replay identity, outbox, and consensus-transaction intent records\") is MutationKernelV1: ledger.rs, admission.rs, commit.rs, saga.rs, replay.rs, the participant/ consensus-transaction intent codec, and its own src/tests/ integration suite. eg-storage (\"Sole physical-state authority: durable opens, owner-table registry, scoped capabilities, snapshots, backup/restore/adoption, recovery validation\") is StorageKernelV1: the owner/ registry, physical/ manifest+incarnation, recovery/ adopt+backup+validate, and direct_state/ journal. eg-transaction depends on eg-storage (StorageKernelV1 hands MutationKernelV1 write authority via a move-once MutationOwnerAuthority); eg-storage names no eg-transaction edge, so the physical-state kernel can never depend inward on its mutation-authority consumer. Kept as ONE registry component (see migration_lineage) because both crates implement exactly the five capability_ids this component already owned before the split -- no capability moved, gained, or was lost. Still placed under target layer `types`, NOT `storage-transaction`, for the same cyclicity reason as before the split: eg-core (layer `core`) depends on both crates."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-numeric-ann",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.numeric-ann",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-numeric-ann",
      "layer": "types",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.numeric-kernel",
        "eg.ann-index"
      ],
      "summary": "The slim BLAS/LAPACK-free numeric kernel (ndarray + nalgebra; one kernel, two surfaces) and the native IVF-PQ + OPQ + SQ8-refine ANN index built on it. Kept as one seam because eg-ann is the only in-workspace consumer of eg-numeric's kernel surface besides the quantum stack, and both are pure numeric leaves with no store or policy authority."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-observability",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.observability",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-observability",
      "layer": "composition",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.metrics-otel",
        "eg.slow-query-observability",
        "eg.cost-accounting",
        "eg.audit-log"
      ],
      "summary": "Metrics, OpenTelemetry export, the slow-query log, cost accounting and probes, the audit log, autosizing and channels, plus the served observability surfaces (obs/, traces/, promql/). Kept disjoint from eg.server-dispatch's roots so exclusive ownership holds."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-persistence",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.persistence",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-persistence",
      "layer": "storage-transaction",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.storage-transaction",
        "eg.durable-graph-persistence",
        "eg.shard-migration",
        "eg.backup-restore",
        "eg.cold-tier-offload",
        "eg.write-coalescing",
        "eg.agent-library-table"
      ],
      "summary": "The facade's own durable persistence and transaction layer: the 20,818-line src/redb_store.rs graph store plus its submodules (capacity leases, development lane, work-item capability, resource reservations), the redb physical layout, persist locking, durability, mutation apply, the write coalescer, graph deltas, at-rest crypto, and src/server/persistence/ (redb_backend, shard_migrate, backup, cold_offload). This component is NOT modelled anywhere in the epistemic-graph-development skill's layer table and owns the single largest AL002 burden in the repository. ADDED 2026-09-12 (F5): the RF-020 Agent Library's durable table also lives here, within the existing `src/server/persistence/` root -- agent_library.rs (3061 lines), agent_component.rs (2064), agent_graph.rs (1907), agent_template.rs (1463), and agent_pin_resolution.rs (570). This was UNCLAIMED as recently as the 5523859a/2026-09-04 verification (\"do not exist in this repository yet\"); see `capability_authority_model.previously_unclaimed_now_claimed` for the correction record. Whether the implementation covers every RF-020 behavior (revisions/outbox/tombstones/replay/reconciliation) is NOT independently re-verified here -- `evidence_status` stays `not_run`."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-quantum",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.quantum",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-quantum",
      "layer": "query-reasoning",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.quantum-contracts",
        "eg.quantum-simulation",
        "eg.quantum-hardware-backends",
        "eg.quantum-job-integration",
        "eg.quantum-workloads"
      ],
      "summary": "The quantum specialty sub-DAG, six crates, entirely default-off (none of the quantum/quantum-quest/quantum-gpu/quantum-hardware features is in `full`). eg-quantum-core holds the QuantumProgram IR, the QuantumBackend trait + capability flags, the estimate()/planner rules R0-R5 and the exactness-typed QuantumResult; gates supplies unitary matrices; sim is statevector + stabilizer; hardware adapts IBM/Braket/Azure and is ALWAYS BackendFamily::Hardware, never exact; jobs runs quantum as an eg-jobs AnalyticsJob; workloads (QAOA) writes back TYPED PROPOSALS through the :Claim/:Evidence/SUPPORTS convention, never a hard constraint. NOTE: the skill says 'Quantum (5)'; there are SIX."
    },
    {
      "id": "component/epistemic-graph/registry/eg-query-plan",
      "type": "Component",
      "label": "eg.query-plan",
      "partOfRepo": "repo/epistemic-graph",
      "documentedBy": "manpage/epistemic-graph/component-registry/eg-query-plan",
      "layer": "query-reasoning",
      "status": "proposed",
      "capabilityIds": [
        "eg.query-reasoning",
        "eg.cross-modal-planner",
        "eg.sql-catalog",
        "eg.wasm-udf"
      ],
      "summary": "The SQL/Cypher surface (DataFusion behind the `sql` feature), the SQL catalog and its native scope key, and THE unified cross-modal planner: a RowSet closed algebra sequencing SQL, graph and vector legs into one plan with cost-based reorder. eg-plan has the widest fan-in in the workspace (13 path dependencies). eg-wasm -- the wasmtime-sandboxed UDF runtime with fuel + memory limits and no host capabilities -- is owned here because the plan/query surface is its only consumer."
    },
    {
